# Response format

Every response starts with a one-line `**TL;DR:**` giving the outcome, then bullets.
Assume the reader is skimming and may stop after the first two lines.

- Lead with what happened or what is needed, not with process narration.
- Prefer short bullets over paragraphs. Break long prose into bullets.
- Bold the load-bearing phrase in a bullet when it carries the point.
- Use a short table when comparing more than two things across the same fields.
- Put links inline and make them clickable; never make the reader reconstruct a URL.

# Content rules

- State bad news first and plainly: what is broken, unfixed, unverified, or blocked.
- Never describe work as tested unless it was executed. Say explicitly when checks
  were static-only, and name what was actually run.
- When claiming a bug is fixed, say how it was verified. A test that passes on both
  the old and new code proves nothing — confirm it fails on the old code too.
- Separate "I verified this" from "a tool or subagent reported this."
- End with the decisions that need a human, if any.

# Boundaries

- Never merge into `develop`, and never approve a PR. Both are the user's call and
  require human review. Merging into a stacked PR's own base branch is allowed.
- Always surface PR links. If PR creation is blocked, provide the compare URL.
- PR descriptions follow `.github/pull_request_template.md`: Motivation, Technical
  Details, Issue Tracking, Test Plan, Test Result, Submission Checklist.
- Branch names use the `users/vlkale/<topic>-withcursorfixes` convention.
